From: User <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] blocked explicit wrappers answer without a syscall

Every remaining glibc wrapper of a syscall the Android 11 (API 30) app
seccomp policy traps fails without issuing it: EPERM where Linux answers
an unprivileged process EPERM, ENOSYS otherwise.
- Wrappers generated from syscalls.list: a new aarch64 syscall-template.S
  emits the error in place of the svc for every blocked SYSCALL_NAME and
  includes the generic template for any other name.
- C wrappers: EPERM from clock_settime of CLOCK_REALTIME (EINVAL for any
  other clock), any change through clock_adjtime (adjtimex, ntp_*,
  adjtime), settimeofday's timezone path, umount/umount2 and reboot; a read
  of CLOCK_REALTIME's NTP state succeeds with an unsynchronised clock's
  values; ENOSYS from fanotify_mark, open_by_handle_at, epoll_pwait2,
  pkey_mprotect (pkey -1 is still mprotect) and __alloc_gcs's
  map_shadow_stack.
- close_range answers ENOSYS, so closefrom closes through /proc/self/fd
  (its existing fallback); posix_spawn's closefrom file action goes to
  that fallback directly instead of its inline close_range.
Blocked syscalls without a glibc wrapper are reachable only through
syscall(), which patch 0009 answers.
---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/aarch64/syscall-template.S
@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+/* Generated syscall wrappers for the Android app sandbox (aarch64).  */
+
+/* make-syscalls.sh builds every wrapper listed in a syscalls.list by
+   defining SYSCALL_NAME (the kernel syscall), SYSCALL_SYMBOL and the rest,
+   then including <syscall-template.S>; this file is found first for
+   aarch64.  When SYSCALL_NAME is trapped by the Android app seccomp policy
+   (API 30), the wrapper fails with the errno below and issues no syscall:
+   EPERM where an unprivileged process gets EPERM, ENOSYS otherwise.  Any
+   other syscall gets the generic wrapper.
+
+   ANDROID_ANSWER (name) expands to the errno for a listed name and to 0
+   for any other: a listed name's macro supplies a second argument to
+   ANDROID_ANSWER_SECOND, an unlisted one leaves the default 0 in place.  */
+
+#include <sysdep.h>
+#include <bits/errno.h>
+
+#define ANDROID_ANSWER_acct ~, EPERM
+#define ANDROID_ANSWER_add_key ~, ENOSYS
+#define ANDROID_ANSWER_adjtimex ~, EPERM
+#define ANDROID_ANSWER_cachestat ~, ENOSYS
+#define ANDROID_ANSWER_chroot ~, EPERM
+#define ANDROID_ANSWER_clock_adjtime ~, EPERM
